# DirectLUSolvers fill/accuracy regression baselines.
#
# Regenerate with:  test_regression --update
# Checked by:       test_regression   (see test/test_regression.cpp)
#
# Fill (nnzL+nnzU) is the load-bearing column: it is a deterministic function of
# the sparsity pattern and the fill-reducing ordering, so a change here is a real
# structural change in the solver, not numerical noise. A large jump is exactly
# the signature of an ordering-direction mistake, which leaves every residual at
# machine precision while inflating 3D factors 250-350x.
#
# nnzL = nnzU = -1 means the solver declined the matrix (info() != Success);
# that is a pinned behaviour too -- a solver that suddenly accepts it has changed.
#
# The +METIS rows additionally pin the METIS BUILD, not just this project's code.
# Nested dissection is randomized, so anything that changes METIS's random stream
# reorders it and moves the fill -- notably rebuilding GKlib with a different
# GKRAND setting (its portable MT19937-64 vs the platform rand()), or changing
# IDXTYPEWIDTH/REALTYPEWIDTH. The rows below were recorded against GKRAND=ON.
# Such a shift is mixed-sign and small in aggregate (measured: mean +0.2% over 19
# matrices, range -17%..+10%), which is how it is told apart from a real
# regression -- those are one-directional and concentrated in the large matrices.
#
# resid is the achieved relative residual, recorded for reference. The gate is
# resid <= max(1e-6, 10 * recorded), so a matrix that legitimately cannot reach
# 1e-6 (singular by construction) is still held to its own accuracy.
#
# time_ms is informational; it is only gated under --check-time.
#
# matrix solver n nnz nnzL nnzU supernodes resid time_ms
